Result of running tool {"request":"Generate the Grad-Shafranov equilibrium equation for an axisymmetric tokamak plasma. The unknown is the poloidal magnetic flux function psi(R,Z) in cylindrical coordinates (R, Z). The elliptic PDE is: R * d/dR( (1/R) dpsi/dR ) + d^2psi/dZ^2 = -mu0 * R^2 * dp/dpsi - F * dF/dpsi, where p(psi) is the plasma pressure profile and F(psi) = R*B_toroidal is the poloidal current function. Use a common analytic model form for the right-hand side (for example the Solov'ev linear source model where the RHS is a linear/simple function such as -(C1 * R^2 + C2)), which yields a linear elliptic problem solvable on a rectangular (R,Z) cross-section domain with psi = 0 on the plasma boundary (Dirichlet boundary conditions). Provide the strong form, weak (variational) form, boundary conditions, and the UFL representation suitable for a finite element discretization.","time-dependent":false,"name":"Grad-Shafranov equation","full-response":"The name of the PDE is [Grad-Shafranov equation].\n\nthe PDE is not time-dependent\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title>Grad-Shafranov Equilibrium Equation (Solov'ev Model)</title>\n<script>\n  window.MathJax = {\n    tex: { inlineMath: [['$', '$'], ['\\\\(', '\\\\)']],\n           displayMath: [['$$', '$$'], ['\\\\[', '\\\\]']] }\n  };\n</script>\n<script id=\"MathJax-script\" async\n  src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\">\n</script>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>Grad-Shafranov Equilibrium Equation (Solov'ev Linear Source Model)</h1>\n\n<h2>General strong form</h2>\n<p>The poloidal flux $\\psi(R,Z)$ in cylindrical coordinates $(R,Z)$ satisfies the\nelliptic Grad-Shafranov equation, where $\\Delta^{*}$ is the Grad-Shafranov operator:</p>\n<p>\n$$\n\\Delta^{*}\\psi \\;\\equiv\\; R\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n\\;=\\; -\\,\\mu_{0}\\,R^{2}\\,\\frac{dp}{d\\psi} \\;-\\; F\\,\\frac{dF}{d\\psi}.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Solov'ev linear source model (strong form)</h2>\n<p>Choosing the Solov'ev closure with $\\mu_{0}\\,dp/d\\psi = C_{1}$ and $F\\,dF/d\\psi = C_{2}$\nmakes the right-hand side a simple linear source, yielding a linear elliptic problem:</p>\n<p>\n$$\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n\\;=\\; -\\left(C_{1}\\,R^{2} + C_{2}\\right)\n\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega \\subset \\{(R,Z)\\}.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Boundary conditions</h2>\n<p>Homogeneous Dirichlet condition on the plasma boundary $\\partial\\Omega$:</p>\n<p>\n$$\n\\psi = 0 \\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Weak (variational) form</h2>\n<p>Dividing by $R$ uses the identity\n$\\tfrac{1}{R}\\Delta^{*}\\psi = \\nabla\\!\\cdot\\!\\big(\\tfrac{1}{R}\\nabla\\psi\\big)$,\nwhere $\\nabla=(\\partial_R,\\partial_Z)$. Multiplying by a test function\n$v \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$, integrating over $\\Omega$, and integrating by parts\n(the boundary term vanishes since $v=0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$):</p>\n<p>\nFind $\\psi \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$ such that for all $v \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$:\n$$\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ\n\\;=\\;\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<p>Bilinear and linear forms:</p>\n<p>\n$$\na(\\psi,v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ,\n\\qquad\nL(v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ.\n$$\n</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n```latex\n% Grad-Shafranov equilibrium equation (Solov'ev linear source model)\n\n% ----- General strong form -----\n% Grad-Shafranov operator:\n\\[\n\\Delta^{*}\\psi \\equiv\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n= -\\,\\mu_{0}\\,R^{2}\\,\\frac{dp}{d\\psi} - F\\,\\frac{dF}{d\\psi}.\n\\]\n\n% ----- Solov'ev linear source model (strong form) -----\n% mu0 dp/dpsi = C1,  F dF/dpsi = C2\n\\[\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n= -\\left(C_{1}\\,R^{2} + C_{2}\\right)\n\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega .\n\\]\n\n% ----- Boundary condition -----\n\\[\n\\psi = 0 \\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega .\n\\]\n\n% ----- Weak (variational) form -----\n% Using (1/R) Delta* psi = div( (1/R) grad psi ), grad = (d/dR, d/dZ).\n% Find psi in H^1_0(Omega) such that for all v in H^1_0(Omega):\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ\n=\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ .\n\\]\n\n% Bilinear and linear forms:\n\\[\na(\\psi,v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ,\n\\qquad\nL(v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ .\n\\]\n```\n\n```python\n# UFL / FEniCS representation of the Grad-Shafranov equilibrium equation\n# (Solov'ev linear source model) on a rectangular (R,Z) cross-section.\n#\n# Strong form:   R d/dR( (1/R) dpsi/dR ) + d^2 psi/dZ^2 = -(C1 R^2 + C2)\n# Divide by R:   div( (1/R) grad psi ) = -(C1 R + C2/R)\n# Weak form:     integral (1/R) grad(psi).grad(v) dx = integral (C1 R + C2/R) v dx\n#\n# Coordinates: x[0] = R (major radius), x[1] = Z (height).\n\nfrom dolfin import (RectangleMesh, Point, FunctionSpace, TrialFunction,\n                    TestFunction, SpatialCoordinate, Constant, DirichletBC,\n                    Function, solve)\nfrom ufl import inner, grad, dx\n\n# --- Mesh over the rectangular (R,Z) cross-section domain Omega ---\nR_min, R_max = 0.5, 1.5   # keep R > 0 (axisymmetric domain)\nZ_min, Z_max = -1.0, 1.0\nnx, ny = 64, 64\nmesh = RectangleMesh(Point(R_min, Z_min), Point(R_max, Z_max), nx, ny)\n\n# --- Function space ---\nV = FunctionSpace(mesh, \"P\", 1)\n\n# --- Trial / test functions ---\npsi = TrialFunction(V)   # unknown poloidal flux psi(R,Z)\nv   = TestFunction(V)\n\n# --- Spatial coordinates: R = x[0], Z = x[1] ---\nx = SpatialCoordinate(mesh)\nR = x[0]\n\n# --- Solov'ev source constants (mu0*dp/dpsi = C1, F*dF/dpsi = C2) ---\nC1 = Constant(1.0)\nC2 = Constant(1.0)\n\n# --- Bilinear and linear forms (weak form) ---\na = (1.0 / R) * inner(grad(psi), grad(v)) * dx\nL = (C1 * R + C2 / R) * v * dx\n\n# --- Dirichlet boundary condition: psi = 0 on the plasma boundary ---\ndef boundary(x, on_boundary):\n    return on_boundary\n\nbc = DirichletBC(V, Constant(0.0), boundary)\n\n# --- Solve the linear elliptic problem ---\npsi_h = Function(V)\nsolve(a == L, psi_h, bc)\n```","html":"\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title>Grad-Shafranov Equilibrium Equation (Solov'ev Model)</title>\n<script>\n  window.MathJax = {\n    tex: { inlineMath: [['$', '$'], ['\\\\(', '\\\\)']],\n           displayMath: [['$$', '$$'], ['\\\\[', '\\\\]']] }\n  };\n</script>\n<script id=\"MathJax-script\" async\n  src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\">\n</script>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>Grad-Shafranov Equilibrium Equation (Solov'ev Linear Source Model)</h1>\n\n<h2>General strong form</h2>\n<p>The poloidal flux $\\psi(R,Z)$ in cylindrical coordinates $(R,Z)$ satisfies the\nelliptic Grad-Shafranov equation, where $\\Delta^{*}$ is the Grad-Shafranov operator:</p>\n<p>\n$$\n\\Delta^{*}\\psi \\;\\equiv\\; R\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n\\;=\\; -\\,\\mu_{0}\\,R^{2}\\,\\frac{dp}{d\\psi} \\;-\\; F\\,\\frac{dF}{d\\psi}.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Solov'ev linear source model (strong form)</h2>\n<p>Choosing the Solov'ev closure with $\\mu_{0}\\,dp/d\\psi = C_{1}$ and $F\\,dF/d\\psi = C_{2}$\nmakes the right-hand side a simple linear source, yielding a linear elliptic problem:</p>\n<p>\n$$\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n\\;=\\; -\\left(C_{1}\\,R^{2} + C_{2}\\right)\n\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega \\subset \\{(R,Z)\\}.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Boundary conditions</h2>\n<p>Homogeneous Dirichlet condition on the plasma boundary $\\partial\\Omega$:</p>\n<p>\n$$\n\\psi = 0 \\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<h2>Weak (variational) form</h2>\n<p>Dividing by $R$ uses the identity\n$\\tfrac{1}{R}\\Delta^{*}\\psi = \\nabla\\!\\cdot\\!\\big(\\tfrac{1}{R}\\nabla\\psi\\big)$,\nwhere $\\nabla=(\\partial_R,\\partial_Z)$. Multiplying by a test function\n$v \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$, integrating over $\\Omega$, and integrating by parts\n(the boundary term vanishes since $v=0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$):</p>\n<p>\nFind $\\psi \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$ such that for all $v \\in H_{0}^{1}(\\Omega)$:\n$$\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ\n\\;=\\;\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ.\n$$\n</p>\n\n<p>Bilinear and linear forms:</p>\n<p>\n$$\na(\\psi,v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ,\n\\qquad\nL(v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ.\n$$\n</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n","latex":"\n% Grad-Shafranov equilibrium equation (Solov'ev linear source model)\n\n% ----- General strong form -----\n% Grad-Shafranov operator:\n\\[\n\\Delta^{*}\\psi \\equiv\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n= -\\,\\mu_{0}\\,R^{2}\\,\\frac{dp}{d\\psi} - F\\,\\frac{dF}{d\\psi}.\n\\]\n\n% ----- Solov'ev linear source model (strong form) -----\n% mu0 dp/dpsi = C1,  F dF/dpsi = C2\n\\[\nR\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial R}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\,\\frac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial R}\\right)\n+ \\frac{\\partial^{2}\\psi}{\\partial Z^{2}}\n= -\\left(C_{1}\\,R^{2} + C_{2}\\right)\n\\qquad \\text{in } \\Omega .\n\\]\n\n% ----- Boundary condition -----\n\\[\n\\psi = 0 \\qquad \\text{on } \\partial\\Omega .\n\\]\n\n% ----- Weak (variational) form -----\n% Using (1/R) Delta* psi = div( (1/R) grad psi ), grad = (d/dR, d/dZ).\n% Find psi in H^1_0(Omega) such that for all v in H^1_0(Omega):\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ\n=\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ .\n\\]\n\n% Bilinear and linear forms:\n\\[\na(\\psi,v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\frac{1}{R}\\,\\nabla\\psi \\cdot \\nabla v \\; dR\\,dZ,\n\\qquad\nL(v) = \\int_{\\Omega} \\left(C_{1}\\,R + \\frac{C_{2}}{R}\\right) v \\; dR\\,dZ .\n\\]\n","python":"\n# UFL / FEniCS representation of the Grad-Shafranov equilibrium equation\n# (Solov'ev linear source model) on a rectangular (R,Z) cross-section.\n#\n# Strong form:   R d/dR( (1/R) dpsi/dR ) + d^2 psi/dZ^2 = -(C1 R^2 + C2)\n# Divide by R:   div( (1/R) grad psi ) = -(C1 R + C2/R)\n# Weak form:     integral (1/R) grad(psi).grad(v) dx = integral (C1 R + C2/R) v dx\n#\n# Coordinates: x[0] = R (major radius), x[1] = Z (height).\n\nfrom dolfin import (RectangleMesh, Point, FunctionSpace, TrialFunction,\n                    TestFunction, SpatialCoordinate, Constant, DirichletBC,\n                    Function, solve)\nfrom ufl import inner, grad, dx\n\n# --- Mesh over the rectangular (R,Z) cross-section domain Omega ---\nR_min, R_max = 0.5, 1.5   # keep R > 0 (axisymmetric domain)\nZ_min, Z_max = -1.0, 1.0\nnx, ny = 64, 64\nmesh = RectangleMesh(Point(R_min, Z_min), Point(R_max, Z_max), nx, ny)\n\n# --- Function space ---\nV = FunctionSpace(mesh, \"P\", 1)\n\n# --- Trial / test functions ---\npsi = TrialFunction(V)   # unknown poloidal flux psi(R,Z)\nv   = TestFunction(V)\n\n# --- Spatial coordinates: R = x[0], Z = x[1] ---\nx = SpatialCoordinate(mesh)\nR = x[0]\n\n# --- Solov'ev source constants (mu0*dp/dpsi = C1, F*dF/dpsi = C2) ---\nC1 = Constant(1.0)\nC2 = Constant(1.0)\n\n# --- Bilinear and linear forms (weak form) ---\na = (1.0 / R) * inner(grad(psi), grad(v)) * dx\nL = (C1 * R + C2 / R) * v * dx\n\n# --- Dirichlet boundary condition: psi = 0 on the plasma boundary ---\ndef boundary(x, on_boundary):\n    return on_boundary\n\nbc = DirichletBC(V, Constant(0.0), boundary)\n\n# --- Solve the linear elliptic problem ---\npsi_h = Function(V)\nsolve(a == L, psi_h, bc)\n"}

